text:
jon: Quick sync: we need a clear recommendation for the JS SDK HTTP layer. Users keep hitting weird errors across Next Edge, Bun, Deno, and older Node. Which approach do we endorse and what onboarding copy do we give?

lisa: Short answer: runtime-detect + no automatic polyfills in edge runtimes. If global fetch exists, use it. For Node <18, provide an opt-in shim path.

thomas: Agreed. In practice I prefer undici for Node server-side since it's maintained and faster than node-fetch. But we must avoid forcing it into browser/edge bundles. So: dynamic import at runtime and mark it optionalDependency.

rina: Typing complexity: many users don't have lib dom in tsconfig. I suggest we publish a tiny types file in the package exposing a minimal SDKFetch signature instead of coupling to DOM libs or undici types. e.g. `export type SDKFetch = (input: string | Request, init?: any) => Promise<any>` (more descriptive in actual d.ts).

jon: Example runtime helper idea (CJS/ESM friendly):
```js
// safeFetch.js
async function resolveFetch() {
  if (typeof globalThis.fetch === 'function') return globalThis.fetch;
  // dynamic import keeps bundlers from pulling undici into browser builds
  try {
    const { fetch } = await import('undici');
    return fetch;
  } catch (err) {
    throw new Error('fetch not available. For Node <18 add undici or upgrade Node.');
  }
}
module.exports = { resolveFetch };
```
This keeps any require/import cheap until runtime.

lisa: Nice. Also add a sync-friendly fallback export that throws a clear error if called synchronously in environments without fetch. Many apps call some SDK helpers at module-eval time and that'll be helpful guidance.

thomas: Bundler note: add conditional exports in package.json so the browser field doesn't resolve the server code that imports undici. Something like: "exports": {"./node": {"require": "./dist/node.cjs", "import": "./dist/node.mjs"}, "./browser": "./dist/browser.mjs"}. And mark undici as optionalDependency so installs don't fail for browser-only consumers.

rina: For TypeScript samples, we should show two tsconfig snippets: frontend (lib: ["dom","es2021"]) and server (lib: ["es2021"]). And include a small d.ts in our package: `/// <reference lib="dom" />` in the types file for the browser entry, plus a separate types file for node entry that references our SDKFetch typedef.

devx-bot: automated note: Prior threads show ~12 incidents of onboarding confusion mentioning "fetch" in the last 6 months. Please add clear doc steps and packages needed.

jon: Onboarding blurb draft:
- Node 18+: nothing to do. SDK uses global fetch.
- Node <=16/17: install undici (npm i undici) and either call `await import('undici')` before using the SDK or call `await initSdkFetch()` provided by our SDK.
- Edge runtimes (Vercel Edge/Cloudflare/Bun/Deno): do nothing; SDK will use global fetch.
- Bundlers: ensure server code uses server entry point to avoid bundling undici into browser builds (see package.json conditional exports).

thomas: Tests: add unit that stubs global.fetch and asserts SDK never imports undici. Another test runs in a Node 16 container with undici installed to ensure our dynamic import path works.

lisa: Make undici optionalDependency and add the runtime check with a clear thrown message linking to docs. Also include the small snippet for CJS vs ESM usage in README.

jon: Small example for README showing both styles:
```js
// ESM
import { initSdkFetch } from '@redwood-inference/sdk';
await initSdkFetch(); // noop on Node 18+/edge

// CJS
const { initSdkFetch } = require('@redwood-inference/sdk');
initSdkFetch().then(() => {});
```

rina: I'll draft the types PR. Keep the runtime helper tiny and well-tested.

thomas: I'm happy to write the package.json export shape examples.

ci-bot: Docs preview available: https://deploy-preview-235.redwood-inference.dev (lint: pass, unit: pass)

jon: Great — let's keep the change minimal and ship it to reduce new user friction. Ping here when PR is up.
